  "image_name": "b3c6fbcc6f071a87bbee74290b222bc2.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how flexible microfluidic devices work and how they control liquid flow. Overall, it presents three themes: what these systems do, what drives fluid motion, and how passive valves regulate transport.\n\nAt the top, flexible microfluidics are shown performing core tasks such as collecting and transporting analytes, separating them, analyzing them electrically, controlling flow direction and speed through channel geometry, and resetting or regenerating serpentine channels by stretching. The drawings emphasize soft, deformable channels that can change function with mechanical deformation.\n\nThe middle row compares several driving forces for fluid flow. Capillary pressure moves liquid through narrow channels using surface tension and wetting effects. Natural pressure appears to come from biological sources such as sweat glands. External mechanical pressure, like a finger press or stretching, can push fluid through the device. Internal negative or positive pressure can also be generated by changes in polymer volume or by gas formed in chemical or enzymatic reactions.\n\nThe bottom row shows passive microvalves that guide or stop flow without active electronics: a capillary bursting valve, a hydrophobic valve, a responsive hydrogel valve that swells or contracts, and a super-absorbent polymer valve that expands to block a channel.",
